  "answer": "Based on current web data retrieved on October 27, 2026, pure water boils on the summit of Mount Everest at approximately **68 °C to 72 °C** (**154 °F to 160 °F**), with standard estimates commonly cited around **69 °C to 70 °C** (**156.2 °F to 158 °F**) [W1], [W2], [W3], [W4], [W5].\n\n* **Atmospheric pressure reduction:** Boiling occurs when water's vapor pressure equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure [W3], [W4]. At the summit of Everest, air pressure drops to roughly one-third (about 33% to 34%) of sea-level pressure—averaging roughly 253 to 260 mmHg, 260 to 300 mbar, or 26.4 to 33.7 kPa [W1], [W3], [W5], [W6]. Because of this significantly thinner atmosphere, the boiling temperature drops by roughly 1 °C for every 300 meters gained [W4].\n* **Seasonal and weather variations:** Summit atmospheric pressure is not constant; it fluctuates with ambient weather and seasons (measuring about 11.5 mmHg higher in midsummer than in midwinter), which causes the actual boiling temperature to vary by about 1 °C [W3], [W7].\n\nThe indexed arXiv papers do not contain physical or barometric data regarding Mount Everest; thus, all figures are provided from current web findings [C1], [C6], [W1]–[W7]."
}